hentication, thus checking the `auth.js` middleware. Examination of the code reveals token verification against a secret key, then a lookup in a user database. The middleware rejects access if the user linked to the token no longer exists.\n\n\n**Diagnosing token authentication failure**\n\nThe application attempts to fetch history records using an outdated token, resulting in a 401. This is because the database was re-seeded, invalidating the old user ID linked to the token. The error handling in the client-side code fails to clear the loading indicator or redirect to the login page after encountering the invalid token.\n\nFurthermore, analyzing the `verify.html` file, a valid user must be authenticated before updating the medicine verification history.\n\n\n**Considering guest user verification**\n\nThe current implementation doesn't save history if the user is unauthenticated because `MedicineHistory` requires a customer ID. Guest users lack this ID.  There's a question of whether local history storage (e.g., in `localStorage`) for guest users is necessary.\n\nSeveral scenarios require handling: an invalid token (401), and a user who verified medicines while not logged in. For 401 errors, clearing the invalid token from `localStorage` and prompting login, or displaying a relevant message in the history table is necessary.\n\nIf a user verifies medicines without being logged in, then their information will not save. Looking at the `history.html` code is necessary.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"grep_search","args":{"MatchPerLine":true,"Query":"api/history","SearchPath":"C:\\Users\\User\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\medcheck-bd\\client","toolAction":"Search api/history in client","toolSummary":"Find all calls to api/history"}}]}
